# tastemaker decisions log

Append-only. Each entry: date, surface, status, and what was applied /
deviated from `.tastemaker/style-lock.md`. `status: pending-review` means
the founder has not signed off yet — do not cite an entry here as design
approval until its status is updated.

---

## 2026-07-27 — apps/swarm-console (the aihu swarm console)

status: pending-review

Applied "Datasheet for a living framework" (style-lock.md's approved
direction) to a new ops-monitoring surface — the founder brief specifically
asked for "instrument-grade, calm, hairline rules not boxes-everywhere,
tabular-nums, Geist Mono for data/labels" on this screen, i.e. the same
character as docs-next's approved direction, carried onto a live dashboard
rather than documentation/marketing.

Decisions:
- **Dark-only.** The lock is light+dark first-class for docs-next; this is
  an all-day monitoring tab (founder brief), so only the lock's dark column
  is implemented — no `:root`/`.dark` split, no light fallback. Tokens are
  copied verbatim from the lock's Color contract + Semantic state token
  tables (dark column): `--bg #14161c`, `--surface #1a1d24`, `--fg #ece9e2`,
  `--accent #e0674b`, `--graphite #aab0bd`, `--border #2b3038`,
  `--muted #a39a92`, `--color-info #8fadc8`, `--color-success #84b898`,
  `--color-warning #d8a848`, `--color-neutral #636a72`.
- **`--color-destructive: #f08070`** is not spelled out in the lock's own
  token table text (only a light-mode oklch comparison is given in the
  amendment prose). Pulled from `packages/css-engine/src/packs.ts`
  (`aihuDefault.dark['color-destructive']`) — the "daihui layer" the lock
  itself cites as source of truth — rather than invented. No new hue: same
  file, same shipped dark destructive token used elsewhere in the repo.
- **Terracotta stays rare.** Used only for the logo dot, the header's live
  pulse (`supervisor_up`), and the agent roster's live-activity pulse
  (`live_active`) — the three sanctioned identity/liveness uses. No primary
  action exists on this read-only console, so that fourth sanctioned use is
  N/A here.
- **State chips**: text color + 1px `currentColor` border on `--surface`
  background, no alpha-tinted fills — the lock's verified "text-safe both
  ways" pairing, unchanged. The REVIEW section's `no-claims` case renders as
  plain neutral-toned TEXT (not a bordered chip), per the founder brief's
  literal wording ("no-claims→neutral text 'claims-unchecked'") — the only
  one of the four verification-tag cases NOT rendered as a chip.
- **No decorative numbering/lettering.** Section labels (`your move`,
  `contracts`, `agents`, `history`) are plain lower-case Geist Mono labels
  on a hairline rule — no leading section numerals (unlike docs-next's
  `.dn-sec-num` "01 —" convention, which the founder's hard constraint for
  this surface explicitly rules out).
- **Geist font files**: byte-for-byte copies of
  `apps/docs-next/public/fonts/{Geist,GeistMono}-Variable.woff2` +
  `GEIST-OFL-LICENSE.txt`, rather than a fresh `geist` npm install — that
  package is not actually a dependency of docs-next either (it isn't in its
  `package.json`/lockfile despite the comment referencing it); the woff2
  binaries already vendored in this repo are the one real self-hosted
  source. Both fonts are self-hosted, same-origin, no CDN. No fallback-stack
  deviation to record.
- **Logo**: the exact path/circle geometry from `brand/favicon.svg`
  (`M 18 30 L 18 56 a 32 32 0 0 0 64 0 L 82 30`, stroke-width 14, dot
  `cx=50 cy=16 r=9`) inlined with `stroke="currentColor"` /
  `fill="var(--accent)"` — the same pattern `apps/docs-next`'s own
  `site-header.aihu` already uses, and the only way the mark can flip its
  `u`-stroke color for a dark surface per `brand/README.md`'s own
  instruction ("For dark surfaces, swap the `u` stroke color… The dot stays
  terracotta in both modes"). The static SVG files under `brand/` hardcode
  light-mode colors and are not directly `<img>`-usable on a dark bg.

No new hex/hue introduced beyond the eleven listed above, all traceable to
`style-lock.md` or `packages/css-engine/src/packs.ts`.

- **Motion — "staggered row entrance" scoped to sections, not rows.** The
  brief asks for staggered entrance on individual DECIDE/REVIEW/CONTRACTS/
  AGENTS/ACTIVITY rows. Implemented and then load-tested live (repeated
  headless-browser screenshots a few seconds apart against the real bus):
  animating each `each`-loop row directly re-triggers the fade-in on every
  ~1s SSE frame, because the row list is a `derived()` that recomputes a
  brand-new array of brand-new objects every frame — a real once-a-second
  flicker across the whole ledger/roster, the opposite of "calm". Rescoped
  the entrance animation to the three top-level `.sc-content` zones
  (your-move / contracts+agents / activity), which mount exactly once
  (gated by `hasData`, which flips false→true a single time). Keeps a
  staggered reveal on first data without a recurring visual defect on a
  screen meant to be watched all day. See the code comment beside
  `.sc-yourmove, .sc-columns, .sc-activity` in `swarm-console.aihu`.

## 2026-07-27 — swarm-console: Tailwind + componentized rebuild (status: pending-review)

Prior console style pass (the entries above, "Datasheet for a living
framework" applied to the ops surface) is **superseded** per direct founder
feedback on the shipped result: "clean it up" / "component style with
tailwind." This is a restyle + restructure, not a new design direction —
the same style-lock tokens, taxonomy, and behavior, rebuilt as componentized
`.aihu` + Tailwind v4 instead of one mega-SFC + hand-rolled utility CSS.
**Status: pending-review** (branch `feat/console-tailwind`, not merged).

- **Tailwind v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`, theme = the lock verbatim.** Every
  lock/packs token realized as a `--color-*` `@theme` entry in the new
  `src/styles/theme.css` (`bg`, `surface`, `ink`, `border`, `muted`,
  `graphite`, `accent`, `info`, `success`, `warning`, `neutral`,
  `destructive` — same 12 hex values as the superseded `tokens.css`, byte
  for byte). `bg-bg text-ink border-border text-success` etc. now exist as
  real Tailwind utilities generated from that one file — the lock realized
  AS the theme, not paralleled by it. Old hand-rolled `tokens.css` (design
  tokens) and the giant `.aihu` `@style` block (the `sc-*` utility classes)
  are both deleted; only what Tailwind can't author survives as raw CSS:
  the `@font-face` block (unchanged, still `base.css`) and the three
  reduced-motion-gated `@keyframes` (`sc-pulse`, `sc-skeleton-pulse`,
  `sc-row-in` — moved into `theme.css`, driven entirely by Tailwind's
  `motion-safe:` variant at each call site, which compiles to the same
  `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)` gate the old CSS used).
  Founder-tuned type scale (`fs-xs`..`fs-xl`, not Tailwind's stock sizes)
  and radius scale preserved by overriding `--text-*`/`--radius-*` in the
  same `@theme` block. The 4px spacing grid needed no custom scale at all —
  it IS Tailwind v4's default `--spacing: 0.25rem` unit (`p-6` = 24px = the
  old `--sp-5`), so every padding/gap value maps to a stock Tailwind number.
- **Dynamic-tone classes are precomputed literal strings, not
  interpolated.** `@tailwindcss/vite` extracts classes by scanning source
  TEXT for literal class-name substrings — it does not evaluate JS, so a
  runtime-built name like `` `text-${tone}` `` is invisible to it (classic
  Tailwind JIT footgun). `src/lib/format.ts` now exports `toneTextClass` /
  `chipClasses` / `neutralTextClasses` / `dotClasses`, each a lookup whose
  every branch is a complete literal string (`'text-success'`,
  `'text-warning'`, …) — both branches always exist verbatim in scanned
  source regardless of which one is live at runtime. Verified via the
  acceptance grep: zero `bg-[#`-shaped arbitrary-color classes anywhere in
  `apps/swarm-console/src`.
- **Componentized — one mega-SFC split into 6 `.aihu` files**
  (`swarm-header`, `your-move`, `contracts-ledger`, `agents-roster`,
  `activity-log`, plus `swarm-console` as the root shell), composed the
  same way `apps/docs-next` composes `<site-header>`/`<theme-toggle>`:
  registered globally in `main.ts`, referenced by tag name in the parent's
  `@template`. Each keeps its own `@state` slice (decide/review/error rows
  in `your-move`, contract rows in `contracts-ledger`, etc.) — the same
  logic the old file had inline, just scoped per file.
- **Data: shared module-level singleton, not component props — a
  deliberate, documented fallback, not an oversight.** The framework has a
  real prop mechanism (`$prop`, plain-curly `<Comp prop={v}>` per
  `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-02-spec-template-attribute-syntax.md`
  §3.3/§10.2) that was the first choice. Not used: propagating one large,
  multi-field reactive `SwarmState` object down through 6 new component
  boundaries is untested reactivity-propagation surface on a compiler that
  had ALREADY bitten this exact app once (a `derived()` left un-lowered, an
  object-literal property with a bare ternary misread as a state decl —
  both documented in the pre-split file and both trap patterns carried
  forward as comments in the new files). `useSwarm()` also opens a real
  `EventSource` per call, so naively calling it in all 6 components would
  open 6 duplicate bus connections. New `src/lib/swarm-store.ts` calls
  `useSwarm()`/`useNow()` once at module scope (ES modules evaluate their
  top level exactly once regardless of import count); every component
  imports that ONE shared instance and derives its own slice from it —
  exactly the fallback this rebuild's brief itself sanctions ("root calls
  once and children read a shared module-level instance, a documented
  pattern choice").
- **Bundle delta (measured, `bun run build`, gzip as reported by Vite):**
  CSS 1.62 kB / gz 0.79 kB → 14.56 kB / gz 4.19 kB (+3.4 kB gz — Tailwind's
  utility CSS for a 6-component app; well under the ~10 KB gz "purge/config
  is wrong" ceiling, so no purge issue). JS 39.21 kB / gz 12.94 kB → 32.80
  kB / gz 11.22 kB (net DECREASE — splitting into smaller focused
  components removed more redundancy than the 5 extra module boundaries
  added).
